<exercise xml:id="ex-venn-diagram-probabilities-tasks">
    <title>Probability and Set Operations</title>
    <introduction>
        <p>
            Let <m>A</m> and <m>B</m> be two events such that <m>P(A) = 0.4</m>, <m>P(B) = 0.7</m>, and <m>P(A \cup B) = 0.9</m>. Use a Venn diagram to help you find the following probabilities.
        </p>
    </introduction>
    
    <hint>
        <p>
            Start by calculating the intersection <m>P(A \cap B)</m> using the inclusion-exclusion principle formula: <m>P(A \cup B) = P(A) + P(B) - P(A \cap B)</m>. Sketch a Venn diagram with two overlapping circles and place this intersection value in the middle. Subtract it from <m>P(A)</m> and <m>P(B)</m> to find the values for the remaining crescent regions, and subtract <m>P(A \cup B)</m> from 1 to find the region outside both circles.
        </p>
    </hint>

    <!-- Part 1 -->
    <task>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Find <m>P(A \cap B)</m>.
            </p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>
                Using the inclusion-exclusion identity:
                <me>P(A \cap B) = P(A) + P(B) - P(A \cup B) = 0.4 + 0.7 - 0.9 = 0.2</me>
            </p>
        </solution>
    </task>

    <!-- Part 2 -->
    <task>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Find <m>P(A^c \cap B)</m>.
            </p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>
                This represents the region containing elements strictly inside circle <m>B</m> but outside circle <m>A</m>:
                <me>P(A^c \cap B) = P(B) - P(A \cap B) = 0.7 - 0.2 = 0.5</me>
            </p>
        </solution>
    </task>

    <!-- Part 3 -->
    <task>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Find <m>P(A - B)</m>.
            </p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>
                This represents the set difference, which isolates the region containing elements strictly inside circle <m>A</m> but completely outside circle <m>B</m>:
                <me>P(A - B) = P(A) - P(A \cap B) = 0.4 - 0.2 = 0.2</me>
            </p>
        </solution>
    </task>

    <!-- Part 4 -->
    <task>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Find <m>P(A^c - B)</m>.
            </p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>
                This isolates elements outside circle <m>A</m>, with any elements belonging to circle <m>B</m> removed. This leaves only the universal region completely outside both circles:
                <me>P(A^c - B) = 1 - P(A \cup B) = 1 - 0.9 = 0.1</me>
            </p>
        </solution>
    </task>

    <!-- Part 5 -->
    <task>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Find <m>P(A^c \cup B)</m>.
            </p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>
                This includes everything outside circle <m>A</m>, combined with the entire area of circle <m>B</m>. It can be computed by taking the complement of the region containing elements strictly in <m>A</m>:
                <me>P(A^c \cup B) = 1 - P(A - B) = 1 - 0.2 = 0.8</me>
            </p>
        </solution>
    </task>

    <!-- Part 6 -->
    <task>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Find <m>P(A \cap (B \cup A^c))</m>.
            </p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>
                Distributing the intersection across the union gives <m>(A \cap B) \cup (A \cap A^c)</m>. Since <m>A \cap A^c = \emptyset</m>, this simplifies down directly to the center intersection region:
                <me>P(A \cap (B \cup A^c)) = P(A \cap B) = 0.2</me>
            </p>
        </solution>
    </task>
</exercise>

<exercise label="ex-streaming-watchlist-probabilities-updated">
    <title>Streaming Platform Media Logistics</title>
    <introduction>
        <p>
            An analysis of a college student's saved media watchlist on a streaming app reveals the following platform statistics:
            <ul>
                <li><p><m>62\%</m> of the saved titles are comedy films.</p></li>
                <li><p><m>45\%</m> of the saved titles are produced by independent studios.</p></li>
                <li><p><m>24\%</m> of the saved titles are comedies produced by independent studios.</p></li>
            </ul>
            If the student selects a title completely at random from this watchlist, what is the probability that it is a non-comedy film produced by a major (non-independent) studio?
        </p>
    </introduction>

    <!-- Part 1: Solving via set laws -->
    <task>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Calculate the probability using standard probability rules, laws, and complement principles. Specify each rule/law/principal as you use it.
            </p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>
                Let <m>C</m> be the event that a selected film is a comedy, and let <m>I</m> be the event that it is an independent studio production. We are given:
                <me>P(C) = 0.62, \quad P(I) = 0.45, \quad P(C \cap I) = 0.24</me>
                We want to find the probability that the film is both a non-comedy (<m>C^c</m>) and from a major studio (<m>I^c</m>). This is represented by the intersection <m>P(C^c \cap I^c)</m>.
            </p>
            <p>
                1. First, find the probability that a film is either a comedy or from an independent studio using the <term>Inclusion-Exclusion Principle</term>:
                <me>P(C \cup I) = P(C) + P(I) - P(C \cap I)</me>
                <me>P(C \cup I) = 0.62 + 0.45 - 0.24 = 0.83</me>
            </p>
            <p>
                2. Next, apply <term>De Morgan's Laws</term> to rewrite our target intersection as the complement of a union:
                <me>C^c \cap I^c = (C \cup I)^c</me>
            </p>
            <p>
                3. Use the <term>Complement Rule</term> to find the final probability value:
                <me>P(C^c \cap I^c) = 1 - P(C \cup I) = 1 - 0.83 = 0.17</me>
                Thus, there is a <m>17\%</m> chance (or a probability of <m>0.17</m>) that the title is a non-comedy from a major studio.
            </p>
        </solution>
    </task>

    <!-- Part 2: Venn Diagram verification -->
    <task>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Verify your calculation by mapping out the regions of a Venn diagram.
            </p>
        </statement>
        
    </task>
</exercise>


<exercise label="SO-dice-complement">
<title>Untititled</title>
   <introduction>
   <p>
      I roll a six-sided die 15 times. 
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <task>
        <statement>
            <p>
                 Find the probability that the number 2 is never observed.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </task>
    
    <task>
        <statement>
            <p>
                 Find the probability that numbers 3 and 4 are both observed at least once.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </task>
   
